<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">German Samoa</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 CE – 1914 CE · capital Apia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's Samoan colony on Upolu and Savaiʻi, governed by Wilhelm Solf through Samoan institutions until New Zealand occupied it in 1914.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a>'s plantation economy since the 1850s through the firm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-c-godeffroy-and-sohn">Godeffroy</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-handels-und-plantagen-gesellschaft">DHPG</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripartite-convention-1899">Tripartite Convention of 1899</a> partitioned the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a>, Germany received the western islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaii">Savaiʻi</a>, and the colony was proclaimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> on 1 March 1900.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-solf">Wilhelm Solf</a>, from 1900 to 1911, ruled indirectly through Samoan chiefs and protected Samoan land from further sale, while abolishing the contested kingship; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mataafa-iosefo">Mataʻafa Iosefo</a> was recognised as paramount chief. Coconut and cocoa plantations used labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Melanesian islands. In 1909 the orator chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lauaki-namulauulu-mamoe">Lauaki Namulauʻulu Mamoe</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mau-movement">Mau</a> a Pule protest and was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> force landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> on 29 August 1914 and took the colony without resistance. It was then governed as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-western-samoa">Territory of Western Samoa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/german-samoa.jpg" alt="German Samoa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Apia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wilhelm Solf, Erich Schultz-Ewerth</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism, Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Samoan, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Samoa</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deutsch-Samoa</dd></dl></aside></div>
